{
  "reasoning": "The lecture proceeds from a technology-level taxonomy of tracking to individual tracking methods, then to additional tracking methods and a closing summary. I cut at explicit topic bridges, such as sentences 16, 32, 50, 65, 71, 92, 99, 110, 125, 140, 153, 162, 173, 182, 187, and 192, so each segment opens with its own lead-in. I split the vision-based and visible-light portions into separate subgoals because the lecturer closes one type before announcing the next. I kept sentence 162 with the GPS segment so the reference to the basic tracking categories is self-contained.",
  "segments": [
    {
      "start": 1,
      "subgoal": "Categorize the main tracking technologies used in VR and AR."
    },
    {
      "start": 16,
      "subgoal": "Describe how electromechanical trackers work and distinguish world-fixed from wearable types."
    },
    {
      "start": 32,
      "subgoal": "Explain electromagnetic tracking using a stationary transmitter and a moving receiver."
    },
    {
      "start": 50,
      "subgoal": "Describe acoustic or ultrasonic tracking and its line-of-sight requirement."
    },
    {
      "start": 65,
      "subgoal": "Explain what vision-based tracking is and list its optical sensor categories."
    },
    {
      "start": 71,
      "subgoal": "Describe infrared tracking and its outside-in and inside-out configurations."
    },
    {
      "start": 92,
      "subgoal": "Classify visible light tracking into fiducial, natural feature, and model-based methods."
    },
    {
      "start": 99,
      "subgoal": "Explain fiducial tracking using artificial landmarks and fiducial designs."
    },
    {
      "start": 110,
      "subgoal": "Explain natural feature tracking using feature descriptors and pose matching."
    },
    {
      "start": 125,
      "subgoal": "Explain model-based tracking using SLAM and its PTAM variant."
    },
    {
      "start": 140,
      "subgoal": "Describe 3D structure tracking using depth sensors and models for pose estimation and augmentation."
    },
    {
      "start": 153,
      "subgoal": "Explain inertial tracking sensors, what they measure, and their limitations."
    },
    {
      "start": 162,
      "subgoal": "Introduce additional tracking methods and explain GPS-based location tracking, including RTK and limitations."
    },
    {
      "start": 173,
      "subgoal": "Explain motion capture as a wearable tracking approach for recording human movement."
    },
    {
      "start": 182,
      "subgoal": "Describe eye-tracking systems and techniques for measuring user gaze."
    },
    {
      "start": 187,
      "subgoal": "Explain hybrid trackers that combine multiple basic tracking methods."
    },
    {
      "start": 192,
      "subgoal": "Summarize the tracking technologies covered and preview the next lecture on VR software."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "reasoning": "I merged the short acoustic segment with the preceding electromagnetic segment because both are transmitter/receiver field-based trackers and the lecture directly compares them. I merged the short vision-based overview with infrared tracking because the overview is mainly a bridge into the first vision-based category. I merged the short visible-light overview with fiducial tracking for the same reason. I merged inertial tracking with the following GPS/other-sensors segment so the inertial discussion forms a more substantial segment about basic inertial plus additional positional sensors. I merged motion capture, eye tracking, and hybrid tracking into one final technical segment because each is a short additional tracking method and together they complete the survey of non-core technologies. The other segments were kept because they already cover a closed technology or closing summary.",
  "segments": [
    {
      "start": 1,
      "subgoal": "Categorize the main tracking technologies used in VR and AR."
